# Validate a dotfiles branch on a clean macOS image.
#
# Nothing is mounted from the host on purpose: the whole point is that the
# guest clones from GitHub the way a fresh machine would, so a step that only
# works because of state already on this laptop fails here instead of on the
# next new machine.

tart clone ghcr.io/cirruslabs/macos-tahoe-base:latest tahoe-validate
tart set tahoe-validate --cpu 6 --memory 12288 --disk-size 90
tart run --no-graphics tahoe-validate &
ssh admin@$(tart ip tahoe-validate)     # admin / admin

# inside the guest, either path:
#   Nix path      -> README, "macOS setup (Darwin)"
#   no-Nix path   -> curl -fsSL .../tools/mise/bootstrap.sh | sh
#                    then: mise run gh-login

tart stop tahoe-validate && tart delete tahoe-validate

# Why tahoe and not golden-gate, on a macOS 15 host: it is the image's DISK
# FORMAT that matters, not the guest's OS version. Golden Gate is published as
# asif, which needs a macOS 26 host, so `tart run` fails with
#
#   Error: Disk format 'asif' is not supported on this system.
#
# and it fails *after* the 30 GiB pull and after `tart set` succeeds, so there
# is no early signal. Tahoe is macOS 26 too and boots here fine (#35) because it
# is published raw. Check the label before pulling:
# `just -g tart-image-format <name>`, or the community/tart/image sheet.

# Runnable: just -g tart-new tahoe-validate tahoe
